## Validating Anything with One Interface

A `Validator` interface lets any type define its own validity rules. A single generic function can validate collections of different validators.

### The Validator Interface

```xiom
interface Validator {
  fn validate(self) -> Bool;
  fn error_message() -> Str;
}

type Age = {
  years: Int;
}

fn Age.validate(self) -> Bool {
  return self.years >= 13;
}

fn Age.error_message(self) -> Str {
  return "Must be 13 or older";
}

type Email = {
  address: Str;
}

fn Email.validate(self) -> Bool {
  return self.address.contains("@") & self.address.contains(".");
}

fn Email.error_message(self) -> Str {
  return "Must contain @ and .";
}

type Password = {
  text: Str;
}

fn Password.validate(self) -> Bool {
  return string.str_len(self.text) >= 8;
}

fn Password.error_message(self) -> Str {
  return "Must be at least 8 characters";
}
```

### The Generic Validation Function

```xiom
fn check_field[T: Validator](field: &T) -> Bool {
  if field.validate() {
    io.println("OK");
    return true;
  } else {
    io.println("ERROR: " + field.error_message());
    return false;
  }
}

fn validate_all[T: Validator](fields: &Vec[T]) -> Bool {
  let all_valid = true;
  let i = 0;
  while i < fields.len() {
    let field = match fields.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => continue };
    if !check_field(field) {
      all_valid = false;
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return all_valid;
}
```

Wait — this requires all fields in the Vec to be the same type. For mixed validators, validate them individually:

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let age = Age{ years: 15 };
  let email = Email{ address: "user@site.com" };
  let password = Password{ text: "weak" };

  check_field(&age);
  check_field(&email);
  check_field(&password);
  return 0;
}
```

### The Validator Pattern in Production

Real systems use validators for: form validation, API input checking, config file verification, data migration integrity. The pattern is always the same: define what 'valid' means per type, then use generic functions to apply validation uniformly.

